values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i100$il$, $il$URGENT for Brad: verify the Frank Pelzer conversation row written into Supabase by hand, before Oliver writes any more$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$blocker$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-17T13:50:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 0, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, true, false, $il${"Brad": "2026-09-18T10:34:27.904Z", "Oliver": "2026-09-17T15:24:32.368Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$URGENT, AND NOTHING ELSE GETS WRITTEN THIS WAY UNTIL BRAD HAS CONFIRMED IT.

On 17 September Oliver wrote a conversation row into Supabase directly through the MCP, rather than through Lovable. It is the InMail to Frank Pelzer, Head of Sales Partner Management (HQ), Vodafone Germany. It renders correctly on the Conversation tab. Rendering correctly is not the same as being right, and Oliver wants it checked before he does any more of them.

THE ROW
  Contact: Frank Pelzer, Vodafone Germany
  Channel: InMail, outbound, from Oliver
  Date: 17 September 2026
  Subject: Versicherungsabo fuer euren Refurbished Shop
  Body: the short German opener about the Recommerce Group refurbished range, the checkout walk, the free month and the monthly cancellable subscription.

WHAT BRAD NEEDS TO CONFIRM, IN THIS ORDER

1. IS IT IN THE RIGHT PLACE. Correct table, correct contact id, correct direction and channel, and every column a downstream read depends on populated rather than null. A row that displays can still be missing the fields nothing shows.

2. DOES EVERYTHING THAT COUNTS TOUCHES SEE IT. PART OF THIS IS ALREADY ANSWERED AND IT IS GOOD NEWS: the Outreach tab shows it as Initial message, InMail, SENT, sent by Oliver, Sent Thu 17 Sept 2026 15:00, under FIRST MESSAGE 1. So the row is visible to the outreach view and is counting as the first message in the sequence. What is still unchecked is the rest: the Sent tab and the volume figure. The sequence position, message N of M, on the next draft for this contact. The day seven reply check, which should now be scheduled off 17 September. The cooldown and the duplicate approach guard. If any of those still read as though nothing was sent, the row exists but does not work.

3. WILL THE NEXT SYNC DUPLICATE IT. This is the one I would check hardest. The row was minted by hand, so it carries no LinkedIn message identifier. i053 has just shown five touches on one contact written in four different id namespaces, with the same connection request recorded twice. A hand written InMail with no platform id is exactly the shape that gets inserted a second time when a sync or backfill next meets the real thing.

4. IS THE INMAIL CREDIT ACCOUNTED. The send spent a credit. If the credit count is derived from touch rows it should have moved; if it is held separately it has not.

5. WHAT THE CORRECT PROCEDURE IS FOR THE NEXT ONE. Oliver will have more of these while Send is broken, see i082. Either Brad confirms this shape and Oliver repeats it exactly, or Brad gives a different route. Guessing twice is how the thing this item exists to prevent happens.$il$,
$il$WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN A SINGLE ROW. This InMail may be the ONLY record of the message that will ever exist. LinkedIn's data export contains DMs and has never contained InMails, so there is no archive to recover it from, and the four hourly watcher has just been shown not to pick up even a native DM, see the Fairphone case on i048. If this row is wrong, it is not wrong until someone fixes it, it is simply gone.

IT IS ALSO THE FIRST LIVE USE OF THE NEW WRITE ROUTE, AND BRAD HAS NOT BEEN TOLD ABOUT IT. His standing rule from 8 September was view only through the MCP: Claude proposes, Lovable applies. That rule is now superseded at guard level 0, with every write logged and shown, but the telling has not happened. This row is the moment it has to, because Brad is about to be asked to verify a write he did not know the route for. Worth doing before he opens the item, not after.

WHAT WOULD MAKE THE WHOLE QUESTION GO AWAY. A supported way to log a message that was sent outside Lovable, from inside Lovable. That is i064, a message sent outside Lovable still gets a follow up, and it is exactly this need. If that existed, Oliver would not be writing rows by hand and none of the five checks above would be necessary. Worth saying plainly to Brad: the manual write is a symptom of a missing button.

ONE OBSERVATION ON THE MESSAGE ITSELF, not a defect. It went as an InMail with a subject line, which is correct per i026, and the German in it is clean, which is the standard i098 is asking the generator to reach. The contrast is worth noting: the hand written message is the one without errors.$il$);
