values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i101$il$, $il$A company is flagged as moved to Monday when it is not, and the flag offers no way to check it$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-17T14:40:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Vodafone Portugal carries the badge Already moved to Monday, with a Restore button. Oliver says there is no Vodafone Portugal in monday, and a check agrees with him.

WHAT I CHECKED. Searching monday items for Vodafone returns no Vodafone record of any kind. What it returns instead is Spusu, giffgaff, A1 Telekom Austria Group, Orange Polska, Fonua, medion and other telcos. So there is no Vodafone company on the board, and the search that found those is matching on similarity rather than on the literal name. That second part is the clue: name based matching between these two systems produces confident false positives, and this is one.

WHY IT IS WORSE THAN AN ORDINARY WRONG FIELD. A suppressed company is invisible work. A wrong status still shows up in a list and gets questioned eventually. A company marked as moved to monday drops out of the worklists on purpose, so nobody goes looking for it and nothing ever contradicts the flag. This particular record is P0, Telco, Portugal, refurbished yes, SIM-free yes, annual devices Large, owner Oliver, product line Pier Protect. That is a serious target that has been silently parked.

IT ALSO COMPOUNDS WITH i046. A company flagged as moved to monday is hidden from the company picker, which is what made spusu look missing. So a wrongly flagged company cannot be worked AND cannot be attached to a new contact, and neither failure announces itself.

WHAT IS NEEDED

1. SUPPRESSION IS KEYED ON A MONDAY ITEM ID, NEVER ON A NAME. Store the id of the monday item the company maps to. If there is no id, there is no suppression.

2. THE BADGE SHOWS ITS EVIDENCE. Name the monday item it claims to map to and link to it, so a wrong flag is falsifiable in one click. Today it asserts and offers no way to check.

3. RUN THE AUDIT. List every company currently flagged as moved to monday and reconcile it against the monday board by id. The number with no matching item is the size of this, and it decides whether this stays a must or becomes a blocker.

4. IF ANYTHING MATCHES BY NAME, STOP IT. At minimum require an exact match plus a confirmation. Prefix and similarity matching will hit every brand family in the lake: Vodafone Germany against Vodafone Portugal against Vodafone Group, and the same for every operator and retailer group.

5. RECORD WHO ARCHIVED IT, WHEN, AND WHY. A flag with no provenance cannot be unpicked. Same gap as i076.

RESTORE EXISTS, WHICH IS THE GOOD NEWS. This record can be fixed in one click. Do not restore in bulk until the audit says which ones are wrong, because some of them will be correct.$il$,
$il$THE RULE BEHIND THE SUPPRESSION IS RIGHT AND SHOULD SURVIVE THIS. A company being worked in monday must not also be worked in the lake, or two people approach the same account from two systems. That is a standing rule and it is not in question. What is in question is how a company is decided to be one of them.

THIS IS THE THIRD PROBLEM WITH THE SAME MECHANISM. i044 says the suppression is applied on some screens and not others. i046 says the company picker hides suppressed companies silently rather than showing them as suppressed. Now i101 says the flag itself can be wrong. Taken together the mechanism is unreliable in all three directions, which is an argument for rebuilding it once on an id rather than patching each symptom.

ON THE VODAFONE FAMILY SPECIFICALLY, and it connects to i099. The lake holds Vodafone Germany, Vodafone Portugal and at least one group level contact, Ben Beasley, filed under the German entity. If anything anywhere matches these by name, the three of them will keep colliding: in the suppression check, in duplicate detection, and in the group rollup. Deciding the Vodafone structure properly once, per i047 and i068, removes the ambiguity that the matching is currently guessing at.

A QUESTION WORTH ASKING BRAD. Where did this flag come from. If it was written by a one off import, the audit is a one time cleanup. If it is recomputed on a schedule, then restoring this record will not hold and it will be re-archived on the next run, which is the version that matters and needs finding before Oliver spends time on restores.$il$);

